# /etc/network/interfaces (in this overlay) hardcodes "eth0" as the name of
# the kiosk's network interface. On real hardware, udev's predictable
# naming (or a different NIC driver) can hand out something else entirely
# (enp0s3, enp2s0, ...), which would leave eth0 undefined and the
# "networking" OpenRC service - and therefore the kiosk service, which
# depends on it - failing to start.
#
# This rule renames the first wired ethernet interface (ATTR{type}=="1" is
# ARPHRD_ETHER) to eth0 regardless of what name udev would otherwise give
# it, so the static config in /etc/network/interfaces keeps working across
# different hardware without per-machine edits.
#
# Wi-Fi interfaces are ARPHRD_ETHER too, so they're excluded explicitly
# (DEVTYPE=wlan) - otherwise on hardware with Wi-Fi (NUCs, laptops) the
# wireless card can win the race for eth0 and the kiosk boots with no
# network.
#
# This assumes single-NIC kiosk hardware, which matches the rest of this
# project. If your target has more than one wired NIC, this rule as written
# will try to rename all of them to eth0 and only one will win - pin to a
# specific device (e.g. by KERNELS==, DRIVERS==, or MAC via ATTR{address})
# instead.
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ATTR{type}=="1", ENV{DEVTYPE}!="wlan", KERNEL!="eth0", NAME="eth0"
